A||||9||N_Man||cradle dolly size?||||1||
Z||000000||N_Man||05-16-2000||01:35 AM||||Hi Everybody<BR>Im wondering the dimensions of peoples homemade dollys.<BR>I know to use 4x4's, caster wheels, and a plywood top but what I cant seem to find is the dimensions.<P>If it makes a difference Its a V6 and I hope to bring the cradle out fully intact, sliding it out of the cars rear (no removing one strut).<BR>thanks||||reg||1
Z||000001||Fiasco||05-16-2000||02:23 AM||||I don't know the dimensions, but I really like Cooter's cradle dolly... <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/003755.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/003755.html</A> ||||reg||14
Z||000002||bHooper||05-16-2000||07:28 AM||||i took a 48"x48" palet and shortenedit about a foot.  then placed 2x4's under the cradle to give me jack points when i needed to manuver the thing around.<P>also i used refrigerator moving wheels that i bought at Lowe's... total cost for my dolly... about $9||||reg||1
Z||000003||Alex4mula||05-16-2000||09:22 AM||||I just did mine two weeks ago when started my 3.4 project (88 Formula). Measured several times and final measurements I used was 39" X 30". I based them on the main square frame around the engine. Used two 4" rotary wheels ($13 ea.) and two 4" stationary wheels ($4 ea.). I suggest to use all rotary. For wood I use 1" X 6" that I had. It was strong enough and held the weight OK. Could use stronger as well. If you leave the struts on you will have to rise the car very high. I tried that and just removed at the end because didn't have the equipment/space to rise it more.||||reg||1
Z||000004||mshill||05-16-2000||12:22 PM||||I bought two furniture dolly's at Home Depot and put them side by side and screwed on two 2x4s the length of them.  Worked perfect and the small castors made it so I didn't have to raise the back end soooo high.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||triker||05-16-2000||12:44 PM||||You should have a good idea of the size from the other posts, and go with all casters, but what I would add is the height.  You'll want it to be at least 10" from the floor to the cradle and even higher if you have enough lifting capacity to clear the engine after it's sitting on the dolly.  You can use blocks of wood to build it up to a comfortable height.  This gives you lot's of room to remove and install the cradle bolts.  I posted before that the best way I found to lift the body is with a 4x4 underneath just forward of the front cradle bolts and 2 come-a-long hoists from an overhead beam to the ends of the 4x4.  Good luck.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Carrolles||05-16-2000||01:43 PM||||Get the front wheels about 6" off the floor (use jack stands or a stack of 2x6s under each tire) so the front apron doesn't hit the ground when you raise the rear end.||||reg||1
Z||000007||N_Man||05-16-2000||11:05 PM||||Thanks guys.<BR>Off to the hardware store.||||reg||1
Z||000008||BN Boomer||05-16-2000||11:34 PM||||Maybe someone needs to come up with a really cool tubular steel cradle dolly.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||6
Z||000009||Spyder||05-17-2000||12:07 AM||||Not to make things simple, <BR>I used  a under the car wooden creeper. The cradle sits down on it and you roll the cradle out the side.||||reg||1
